# Line endings, normalised once so they stop being noise.
#
# This repo had no .gitattributes, and it cost real time. Files were stored with
# a mix of LF and CRLF, and a Windows editor rewriting one on save produced a
# diff touching every line — 53 files showed as modified while only 6 had any
# content change, and one scripted edit produced a 490-line whitespace diff that
# had to be spotted and undone before it was committed. Worse, a genuine change
# hides inside that noise: nobody reviews a file where every line is marked.
#
# `text=auto` makes git decide per file: anything it detects as text is stored
# LF in the repository and checked out with the platform's native ending. Nobody
# has to configure core.autocrlf, and it cannot drift between machines.
* text=auto

# Explicit for the sources here, so detection is never the deciding factor.
*.py      text
*.ts      text
*.tsx     text
*.js      text
*.jsx     text
*.json    text
*.md      text
*.yml     text
*.yaml    text
*.sql     text
*.css     text
*.html    text
*.toml    text
*.cfg     text
*.ini     text
*.txt     text

# Shell scripts and the container entrypoint MUST be LF regardless of platform:
# a CRLF shebang line makes the kernel look for an interpreter named "sh\r",
# which fails with a "no such file or directory" naming a file that plainly
# exists. scripts/start.sh is the Docker entrypoint, so that failure would be a
# broken deploy, not a local annoyance.
*.sh      text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Binary — never touched.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.mp3     binary
*.wav     binary
